5:["$","article",null,{"className":"max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 prose prose-slate","children":[["$","h1",null,{"children":"How FastScience! Cuts Research Time with Graph AI"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"text-xs text-slate-500 not-prose","children":"2026-05-25"}],"$Ld"]}]
d:[["$","p",null,{"children":"The instinct in 2026 is to throw a bigger language model at any research problem. That instinct is wrong, and it costs you weeks of compute."}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"Here is the stack we actually use, and why each layer earns its place."}],"\n",["$","h2",null,{"children":"Layer 1: Neo4j as the causal backbone"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":["We do not store papers — we store ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"entities and causal arcs"}]," extracted from papers. A typical kwiKBio knowledge graph for a single domain holds on the order of tens of thousands of entities and hundreds of thousands of typed arcs (see the ",["$","a",null,{"href":"/blog/microbial-consortia-prism9-case-study","children":"microbial consortia case study"}]," for exact figures from one production graph). Arc types matter: ",["$","code",null,{"children":"regulates"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"inhibits"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"co-occurs-with"}],", and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"causally-precedes"}]," are not interchangeable."]}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":["Neo4j is the right database here because ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"path queries are first-class"}],". The questions we ask — \"what regulates X two hops away from Y?\" — are a few-line Cypher query on Neo4j and a sprawling SQL join on Postgres."]}],"\n",["$","h2",null,{"children":"Layer 2: Sub-linear association mining"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"Sub-Linear Association Mining (SLAM) is a body of academic work — notably advanced by Evan Steeg — designed to find higher-order correlations in multidimensional data that pairwise statistics miss. We use a SLAM-style approach to surface candidate causal arcs the literature has not yet annotated."}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":["The output is always a ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"hypothesis"}],", never a claim. SLAM-style mining proposes \"these three entities co-cluster in a meaningful fraction of relevant studies\"; the rest of the pipeline tests whether that co-clustering is causally informative."]}],"\n",["$","h2",null,{"children":"Layer 3: PRISM-9 reduction"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":["Most well-posed research problems share a useful property: they're ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"dominated by a small number of entities"}],", often ≤9. The rest of the graph contributes noise."]}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"PRISM-9 forces that reduction. It picks the 9 entities whose joint variance explains the most outcome variance for the current question, then prunes everything else. This is the step that makes the next layer tractable."}],"\n",["$","h2",null,{"children":"Layer 4: Cube-27 expansion"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"Each of the 9 dominant entities expands fractally into 3 sub-factors, giving a 3×3×3 cube of 27 leaf variables. The geometry preserves causal locality, so adjacent cells correspond to causally adjacent sub-factors."}],"\n",["$","h2",null,{"children":"Layer 5: QUBO solving"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"With 27 binary variables (active / inactive sub-factor), path planning becomes a Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization. That is a problem class quantum annealers (such as D-Wave Advantage at 5,000+ qubits) are designed to address."}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":["We get a near-optimal causal path in ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"seconds"}]," on quantum hardware when available; classical simulated annealing handles it in ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"minutes"}]," as fallback. Either way, the user sees ranked experiments quickly."]}],"\n",["$","h2",null,{"children":"Why not just bigger LLMs?"}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"Because LLMs hallucinate causal arcs, and grounding them in your specific domain manual requires retrieval plumbing that often costs more than the model. The graph layer eliminates a class of failure modes that pre-training does not fix."}],"\n",["$","p",null,{"children":"LLMs remain in the loop — as our natural-language interface and our summarizer. They do not drive the science."}]]
